A||||7||MooCow||How to tell if ball joints are bad?||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||09-12-2001||01:08 AM||||Just like the heading says.  How do I know if they are bad??  My car handles for crap on the highway.  I let my friend drive my car and he was going 70 and he switched lanes fast and the back end of my nearly rolled.  It was so bad.  The front stayed level but the back end just twisted to one side when he did that.  Anyone know what is causein that??  My dogbone bad or engine mounts bad??<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mickey_Moose||09-12-2001||10:33 AM||||check your rear struts and mounts - the struts are probably worn or the top mounting is broken<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||09-12-2001||10:48 AM||||Hey Moo I have the same problem.  I replaced all my bushings to poly and still have the problem.  It looks like the stuts are bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Denny||09-12-2001||02:31 PM||||Can also be caused be worn/loose motor and transmission mounts.<P>I had an 85 GT that the previous owner had not torqued the mounts to spec after rebuilding and replacing the engine, and it did exactly that. Stopped doing it after I torqued all the mounts.<P>------------------<BR>Denny Lambert<BR>Custom 1986 SE<BR>Stock 1986 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SEGTfront.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||NEPTUNE||09-12-2001||04:35 PM||||if you want to check ball joints anyway, jack up one front wheel about 3-4", put a lever (4' pipe) against the bottom sidewall of the tire, and apply pressure. you should not feel/hear any popping or looseness.<BR>that is probably not your problem, though... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||09-12-2001||09:30 PM||||Hey Ken!<BR>You can do what I did!<BR>Let it go till it falls apart!<BR>Seriously though, if it is the rear, you could also have bad wheel bearings as well as bad ball joints...<BR>Use a floor jack, put it under the control arm, remove the wheel and push and pull different parts to see what is loose...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000006||GarethWright||09-12-2001||10:51 PM||||Jack the wheel(s) in question off the floor, place hands at 12 and 6 o'clock on the wheel (top and bottom) pull the top, push the bottom, pull the bottom, push the top, if the wheel is fairly loose, its probably the ball joints.<BR>Put hands at 9 and 3 o'clock, push and pull if its loose that way, its probably the tie rods.<BR>On mine, on the front, it was both.<BR>Good luck with it<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||09-16-2001||10:09 PM||||To find a bad ball joint you have to squeeze the stud portion into the socket and watch very carefully for any movement;if you detect any movement the part is bad and you'll notice it when driving.<BR>The only tool I know of that'll work for this is the very large set of Channel-Lock (waterneck-style pliers.<BR>A worn ball joint will appear to be OK in the tests described in the earlier posts,unless they are VERY worn,and would be creaking at that point anyways.||||reg||1
